***********************************
Virsh undefine removes only the VM definition from libvirt.
Your actual VM disk and OS are usually still safe.

Since you are using Ceph RBD, your VM data is most likely still present in: vm-pool/vm1-101 or similar RBD image.

First Verify RBD Image Still Exists

# rbd ls vm-pool

If you still see:  vm1-101

then your VM disk is safe.

Also Verify Image Contains Data

# rbd info vm-pool/vm1-101

Check:

size
objects
timestamps

virsh undefine vm101 removes:

libvirt XML definition
VM metadata

It does NOT remove:

Ceph RBD image
qcow2/raw disks
guest OS data

unless special flags used.

***** Recovery Method ******

You simply need to recreate VM definition.

Easiest Recovery Method
Step 1 — Create Empty XML Skeleton
virt-install \
--name vm101 \
--memory 1024 \
--vcpus 2 \
--disk none \
--network network=default,model=virtio \
--os-variant alpinelinux3.22 \
--import \
--dry-run \
--print-xml > vm101.xml

  Step 2 — Edit XML
  vi vm101.xml

Replace disk section with your Ceph RBD disk.

 <disk type='block' device='disk'>
  <driver name='qemu' type='raw' cache='none' io='native' discard='unmap'/>
  <source dev='/dev/rbd1'/>  ==> Verify this
  <target dev='vda' bus='virtio'/>
 </disk>
  
Example:

Step 3 — Define VM Again
virsh define vm101.xml

Step 4 — Start VM
virsh start vm101

